## Overview
Create a custom Medusa middleware file to increase the Express body parser limit for file upload endpoints from the default to 25MB.

## Technical Background
Medusa.js v2 uses Express under the hood. The body parser middleware controls the maximum size of HTTP request bodies. Without custom configuration, it uses small defaults (~100KB-1MB) which cause large file uploads to fail.

Medusa v2 provides `defineMiddlewares` from `@medusajs/medusa` to configure per-route middleware settings including body parser limits.

## File to Create
```
table-clay-store/src/api/middlewares.ts
```

## Implementation Code
```typescript
import { defineMiddlewares } from "@medusajs/medusa"

export default defineMiddlewares({
  routes: [
    {
      // Main upload endpoint for product images
      matcher: "/admin/uploads",
      bodyParser: {
        sizeLimit: "25mb"
      }
    },
    {
      // Catch sub-routes like /admin/uploads/:id
      matcher: "/admin/uploads/*",
      bodyParser: {
        sizeLimit: "25mb"
      }
    },
    {
      // Presigned URL endpoint for direct S3 uploads
      matcher: "/admin/uploads/presigned-urls",
      bodyParser: {
        sizeLimit: "25mb"
      }
    },
  ],
})
```

## How It Works
1. Medusa automatically loads `src/api/middlewares.ts` on startup
2. The `matcher` property uses glob patterns to match request paths
3. `bodyParser.sizeLimit` sets the Express json/urlencoded/raw body parser limit
4. The limit applies to both JSON payloads and multipart form data

## Testing Instructions
1. Start the backend: `cd table-clay-store && yarn dev`
2. Open admin dashboard at http://localhost:9000/app
3. Navigate to Products > Any Product > Media
4. Attempt to upload a file larger than 1MB but smaller than 25MB
5. The backend should NOT return a 413 (Payload Too Large) error
6. Check server logs for any body parser errors

## Notes
- This only fixes the BACKEND limit
- The frontend (admin dashboard) also has a limit that needs separate patching
- 25MB was chosen as a balance between high-quality images and upload performance
- Railway has a 30-second request timeout, so very large files may still timeout on slow connections

